findings.description:
In blogger.env.j2, secrets are rendered as bare values:
  TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN={{ blogger_telegram_bot_token }}
  CALLBACK_HMAC_SECRETS={{ publish_callback_hmac_secrets }}
For a systemd EnvironmentFile=, bare values (no quotes) are correct — systemd reads the file as key=value pairs and does not do shell-style quote stripping. The values are read verbatim. However, if a token ever contains special characters (spaces, #, \n), an unquoted render could truncate the value at the first space or treat # as a comment. Bot tokens from @BotFather are alphanumeric+colon and will never contain these characters. HMAC secrets generated via `openssl rand -hex 32` are pure hex. So the risk is theoretical for current value formats, but it is good practice to note for future maintainers.
